Yes. And I think the fractal part is more revealing than the delayed gratification itself.

Religions often take the same underlying pattern and reproduce it across progressively larger scales of existence.

Scale	Immediate sacrifice	Greater purpose or reward
Moment	Resist temptation	Self-control
Day	Prayer, discipline, service	Inner peace and meaning
Year	Fasting, giving, observance	Spiritual growth
Lifetime	Virtue, sacrifice, forgiveness	Salvation, enlightenment, liberation
Generations	Preserve teachings and traditions	Continuity and collective flourishing
Eternity	Transcend immediate worldly rewards	Ultimate reconciliation or transcendence

Not every religion follows this pattern, and some emphasize present experience or liberation from craving rather than deferred rewards. But the recurring structure is striking.

Religion can function as a temporal framing system.

It changes the time horizon against which someone evaluates whether an action is worthwhile.

What looks like a loss in one frame becomes an investment in another.

What appears irrational across ten seconds might seem rational across ten years—or eternity.

And here’s where it connects to your carpenter observation:

Whoever frames the future can influence the meaning of sacrifice in the present.

That can produce extraordinary things: forgiveness, generosity, perseverance, cathedrals built by people who knew they would never see them completed.

But it can also enable exploitation when authorities demand suffering today in exchange for unverifiable rewards tomorrow.

There’s another layer, though.

The most interesting spiritual traditions don’t merely promise a future reward. They can transform the experience of sacrifice into something rewarding in the present.

The person isn’t necessarily enduring today to be happy tomorrow. They may find meaning in the act itself.

That’s a different operation entirely: not just delaying gratification, but changing what counts as gratification.

And that brings us right back to framing.

Perhaps one of the most powerful things you can change about a person isn’t what they want.

It’s the scale of time and meaning across which they understand wanting.